collar criminal  defense attorneys for 15 years  I have friends who were federal judges  in my experience and in my conversations  with them the one thing they tell me  over and over again is in a normal  non-political non media heavy case these  are two three year investigations it's a  slow process and again that that  dovetails with my experience in large  civil litigation you've got because you  know if you watch like you know law and  order TV shows you know you you flip  witnesses in twenty minutes and then get  them to testify these are as we as we've  seen from like the manna for indictment  that came down these are bad guys who  are associated with the inner circle of  the president and one of the questions  one of the duties that robert muller has  is to prosecute everybody who's  committed a crime while making the best  possible case against the ringleaders  and and all the way up to the office of  the president so take take manna fort  for example you might think that as a  matter of you know kind of law and order  type strategy oh we'll just give man  afford immunity and have him flip and  testify against the president and then  boom you're done but but part of what  Muller is trying to figure out is how  well can I make the case without having  to give man afford immunity because he's  committed uh uh again he's allegedly  committed according to the indictment  tens to hundreds of millions of dollars  in tax fraud in in except in in  laundering money from the Ukraine and  those are those are allegations I want  to be clear on that but if that's the  case and and clearly indictments were  handed down so Muller thinks that  there's a strong probability that that's  the case you need to you need to figure  out do I let somebody who has defrauded  the u.s. out of hundreds of millions of  dollars who I think has defrauded the  u.s. out of hundreds of millions of  dollars walk to make a political case  against the president and all of that  requires basically doing your homework  collecting as much information going  through it and if the  and the Republican strategy has been to  try and convince the public that it  should be the opposite right like oh  well we should have an answer by now and  the investigation should be done it's a  slow process so one of the common  talking points from the nothing to see  here crowd which exists on both the left  and right for different reasons but we  don't have to get into that aspect of it  today is listen David everything that's  coming out so far relates to possible  crimes that have nothing to do with  collusion between the Donald Trump  campaign and Russian actors it's  financial fraud committed before these  people Paul Manafort for example worked  for Trump or whatever the case may be  can we first define collusion itself is  not a crime so what sorts of specific  crimes might be possible under this  general umbrella of colluding with  Russia yeah so you're right and the  nothing to see here crowd is technically  correct that collusion isn't a crime but  what is a crime is conspiracy and and in  particular there's a statute it's 18 US  Code section 371 that is conspiracy to  defraud the United States and it's a  really really say it's a three-line  statute it says if two or more persons  conspire to commit any offense against  the United States or to defraud the  United States or any agency thereof for  any purpose and one or more of those do  any act to effect the conspiracy then  they've committed the crime and it's and  it carries up to it of a five year  prison sentence manna fort is charged  with conspiracy to defraud the US it's  true that a lot of the lot of the counts  relate to tax fraud as we've said but  but the idea that you would dismiss that  as not being significant is is kind of  beyond me and and I and I want to be  clear there's there are multiple cases  there's a Supreme Court case that goes  back to 1924 it's called hammerschmidt  the u.s. that explicitly says that  conspiracy to defraud the United States  includes a conspiracy to interfere with  or obscure  any lawful government function right and  free and fair elections are very very  plainly a lawful government function if  that wasn't clear then in 1996 you might  recall and this is you know sort of good  ammunition you know for the the Trump  supporting crowd there were allegations  that the Clinton the Bill Clinton  re-election campaign was receiving undue  influence from China with respect to the  1996 election there was a financier from  Little Rock named Charlie tree I was a  Clinton fundraiser and he was indicted  and pled guilty and he didn't plead  guilty to 18 USC 371 he people had  guilty to violations of election list  but he was charged under 18 USC 371 and  again in that case there was no evidence  that the Chinese attempt to interfere  went anywhere high up in the in the  Clinton campaign organization but it was  investigated and that was the the  statute that Republicans thought at the  time was appropriate to use if if a  foreign government was trying to  interfere with with a US election and  you know we took it seriously in 1996 we  should take it seriously now hey well  listen all of this gets me to the real  question which is when are we gonna lock  her up what it actually gets me to  though is is the different question  which is when you talked about this idea  of really the path forward if there are  big discoveries from this would be  political wins such that Donald Trump is  impeached or sort of forced to resign  what happens to Mike Pence in that  situation in other words does it just  become pence as president or what would  it take for pens to be as implicated as  Trump in whatever so so here's here's  the kind of lengthier answer that we do  we do a recurring segment on opening  arguments called yodel mountain and the  reason we call it that is because I was  asked that very question again you know  week one after Donald Trump assumed  office and in light of the of the Jared  Kushner appointment you know  what's it gonna take to impeach Donald  Trump and my answer was you know do you  remember the old prices right game with  the yodeler going up the mountain and  every time you missed by a dollar the  yodel would you know the little mountain  climber would would climb up and when  you missed by a total of so many dollars  he would reach the top of the mountain  and fall off and that's the way to think  about this sort of drip drip drip of  criminal activities right we know the  president has committed crimes already  we know that according to Republicans  it's not serious enough for them to  think that that those crimes are  impeachable there is a point where the  yodeler will fall off the mount there is  a point where that cumulative effect is  so great that even Republicans in safe  districts are gonna are going to turn on  the president obviously we're not there  yet now how does that affect Mike Pence  as far as I can tell there is no  evidence that connects Mike Pence up  with any of the public investigation to  date I would  wherever you put the probability of  Trump being impeached that you have to  set the probability of that applying to  Mike Pence at almost zero from from what  we know right now so you see you have to  be willing to say as and again I'm a  left-of-centre Democrat as somebody who  does not want to see Mike Pence be  present in the United States that I'll  accept that  I'll accept that outcome in order to not  have somebody in office who as far as I  can tell has no public service what so a  trite cares only about enriching himself  his immediate circle of cronies and his  friends yeah well now we're getting to  more of what I agree with is the  political analysis which is that yeah I  believe the best scenario for the left  is probably from staying not resigning  or being impeached riddled and scandal  unable to accomplish or at least able to  accomplish as little as possible and and  the public spectacle of that leading to  a major loss for both Trump and the  Republicans in 18 and 20 because I think  that if Mike Pence becomes president he  becomes  way less scandalous and his sort of  standard right-wing ideologue  perspective actually gets a lot of  really horrible stuff done I I I respect  that point of view that was the point of  view I held in January sort of pre you  know pre inauguration it it I have to  tell you I find it knowing right because  the predicate of what you've just said  is if Mike Pence is president we have a  much greater risk of aberrant political  policies being elected because Mike  Pence is not ridden by scandal and he is  a much more competent political  operative I'm willing to take that risk  in my plan again I understand how people  can come out differently I'm willing to  take that risk at this point because  Mike Pence is at least a career civil  servant right I disagree with his  definition of civil service but he has  he's somebody who operates within the  political process and I believe that he  will be deterred or believe that he has  a stronger chance of being deterred by  not engaging in blatantly illegal  conduct and not you know sort of setting  up these irresolvable conflicts then  then Trump is and and I think he cares  about things beyond just lining his  pockets and his and his family's pockets  it I get that it's a tough call but I  mean this is really you know as somebody  who's you know been here for two decades  and politically active four three like  this is not normal right it is not it's  not usual to have courts handing down  injunctions blocking executive orders at  eleven o'clock at night on a Thursday  right like it it is not usual to have  you know two competing courts issuing  two differing rulings on two  interpretations of statutes right this  is we've been we've been put into a  position that you know like we survive I  like a you know and again I was pretty  anti George W Bush but but you know we  survived that with our institutions  intact we survived George HW Bush we  survived Ronald Reagan I feel like this  is much more of an  an existential threat hard to argue  against that for sure we've been  speaking with Andrew Torres check out  the opening arguments show thanks so  much for talking to us Andrew we will  definitely do it again  David it's always fun to be on the show  thank thank you so much for having me on  having me back

